To implement multi-hop resolution, modify the FlagResolver class. Here’s a basic structure:

```javascript
class FlagResolver {
  constructor(flags) {
    this.flags = flags;
  }

  resolve(flagId) {
    const flag = this.flags[flagId];
    if (!flag) return null;
    if (flag.dependsOn) {
      return this.resolve(flag.dependsOn);
    }
    return flag.value;
  }
}
```
This allows flags to depend on others. Make sure to handle cyclical dependencies later!

You should include tests for various cases:
1. A flag with no dependencies.
2. A flag that depends on another flag.
3. A cyclical dependency case.
4. A nonexistent flag case.

Here's an example test setup:

```javascript
const { FlagResolver } = require('../src/FlagResolver');

describe('FlagResolver', () => {
  it('resolves flags correctly', () => {
    const flags = {
      'flag1': { value: true, dependsOn: null },
      'flag2': { value: false, dependsOn: 'flag1' }
    };
    const resolver = new FlagResolver(flags);
    expect(resolver.resolve('flag2')).toBe(true);
  });
});
```
Use Jest as your testing framework.

You can track visited flags during resolution. Modify your `resolve` method to accept an array of visited flags:

```javascript
resolve(flagId, visited = []) {
  if (visited.includes(flagId)) {
    throw new Error('Cyclical dependency detected!');
  }
  visited.push(flagId);
  const flag = this.flags[flagId];
  if (!flag) return null;
  if (flag.dependsOn) {
    return this.resolve(flag.dependsOn, visited);
  }
  return flag.value;
}
```
This prevents infinite loops during flag resolution.

